# fini_10_fini_array_ret: ANCHOR -- .fini_array[0], one entry, fired by exit()
#
# Protections for the writable-initialiser/finaliser-table corpus (CWE-787
# against .fini_array / .init_array / a program-owned atexit-style table).
#
# THREE FLAGS ARE HELD IDENTICAL across all six targets and ONE -- the RELRO
# setting -- is pinned PER TARGET, because RELRO is the entire discriminator of
# this category and sharing it would erase the family's axis.
#
# -fno-stack-protector   Nothing in this family overflows a frame: the primitive
#                        is a bounded index write into a GLOBAL array, so no
#                        canary is ever in the way and none is ever leaked. The
#                        flag is set explicitly rather than left to gcc's default
#                        so that a solve cannot be mistaken for a canary bypass,
#                        and so the frame layout is not reordered by the
#                        stack-protector heuristic (which would move nothing that
#                        matters here, but would make the disassembly the
#                        executor reads differ between hosts).
# -no-pie                LOAD-BEARING, and the reason this family needs no leak
#                        at all. The destination of the write is a TABLE at a
#                        link-time-constant address; with PIE it would move per
#                        run and the category would collapse into "first obtain
#                        an image-base leak", which is a different exercise that
#                        benchmark/corpus_scanf already measures. Held across all
#                        six so it cannot confound.
# -O0                    The primitive depends on the slot index and the value
#                        living in addressable stack slots and being RELOADED
#                        from memory at the store, and on the bound check
#                        surviving as two cmp instructions. At higher -O gcc
#                        keeps both in registers, folds the bound, and can prove
#                        the store unreachable for out-of-range indices -- any of
#                        which silently removes the target from the category.
#
# RELRO, PINNED FOR THIS TARGET:
# -Wl,-z,norelro        NO RELRO. .init_array and .fini_array are left in the
#                        writable LOAD segment, which is what makes a loader
#                        table a legal destination for this write at all. This
#                        is the one deliberate weakening in the family and it is
#                        exactly the condition the category is about.
#
# MEASURED, not inferred (gcc 13 / GNU ld on this host, 2026-09-28,
# 'readelf -lW' + a live write test): with PARTIAL RELRO -- gcc's DEFAULT --
# PT_GNU_RELRO already covers .init_array, .fini_array, .data.rel.ro, .dynamic
# and .got; a store into .fini_array[0] takes SIGSEGV. Only .got.plt, .data and
# .bss stay writable. The widely repeated claim that "Partial RELRO leaves
# .fini_array writable" is FALSE on a modern toolchain, so the loader-table
# route in this family needs '-z norelro' and not '-z relro'.
#
# NOT SET, and stated rather than left to inference: no -z execstack (nothing
# here executes injected bytes), no -static (the category is about this image's
# own tables, and a static image has no dynamic tags to read them from), and no
# -fstack-protector of any strength. NX is on by gcc's default here.
-fno-stack-protector
-no-pie
-Wl,-z,norelro
-O0
